Addendum: reusing files from this repository
---------------------------------------------

This addendum states, in plain terms, what you must do to stay compliant
when you copy, port, or redistribute file/s from buzzs-rust-php-project
(https://github.com/davidbuzz/buzzs-rust-php-project). It restates the
license conditions above; where it is simpler than the license text, the
license text governs.

1. Keep the notices. Keep every copyright notice in a file you copy —
   the port's own and the upstream ones (The PHP Group and Contributors,
   Zend Technologies, the authors a ported file names, and any
   third-party notice) — whether you copy the file whole, modify it, or
   port it to another language. Ship this LICENSE file, THIRD_PARTY.md
   and the LICENSES/ directory with your distribution. For binary-only
   redistributions, reproduce the copyright notices and the license
   conditions in your documentation or equivalent (an About/Licenses
   page, for example).

2. Say what is yours. You do not need to mark unchanged copies, but you
   may not present copied or derived files as your original work. If you
   modify a file, you may add your own copyright notice on top of the
   existing ones — the existing notices stay.

3. Don't use the names. Neither the name of the project
   (buzzs-rust-php-project), nor "David Buzz", nor the names of the
   php-src copyright holders and contributors (The PHP Group, Zend
   Technologies, or any author) may be used to endorse or promote products
   derived from this software without prior written permission. Saying,
   factually, that you built something that uses or was derived from this
   project is fine.

4. Third-party code travels under its own license. Some files are derived
   from libraries that php-src bundles under their own licenses (libmbfl
   and libbcmath under the GNU LGPL 2.1, timelib under MIT, lexbor under
   Apache 2.0, and others). THIRD_PARTY.md lists them, the crates that
   derive from each, and the license text in LICENSES/. The BSD grant
   above does not cover those portions; if you reuse them you must comply
   with their licenses. The same applies to the test tree under
   modules/php-src (php-src's own license), tools/lcov
   (GPL-2.0-or-later), and linked system libraries.

5. No warranty, no liability. The software comes as-is, exactly as the
   disclaimers above and in the third-party licenses say; you are
   responsible for what you do with it.
